{:edb/db-before-t 2393 :edb/db-after-t 2394 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535706 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:40:00.957Z" 13194139535706 true] [17592186124177 1100 "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4)$." 13194139535706 false] [17592186124177 1100 "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186124177 1145 "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v$.\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} & = \\langle  1 , 4 \\rangle  - \\langle  0 , 1 \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  1 , 3 \\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & = \\frac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\Vert  \\overrightarrow{AB} \\Vert} \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2} + {3}^{2}}} \\langle  1 , 3 \\rangle \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\langle  1 , 3 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(x, y) &= \\langle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(3x^{2} + 4xy), \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(3x^{2} + 4xy)\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle 6x + 4y, 4x\\rangle  \\\\\n\\nabla g(0, 1) &= \\langle 6 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 1, 4 \\cdot 0\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle 4, 0\\rangle \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_{u}^{\\prime }(0, 1) &= \\nabla g(0, 1) \\cdot u \\\\\n&= \\langle 4, 0\\rangle  \\cdot \\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535706 false] [17592186124177 1145 "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186124178 1100 "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)$?" 13194139535706 false] [17592186124178 1100 "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186124178 1145 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- \\nabla f(x, y) &= -\\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(\\ln (x^{2}y))i - \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(\\ln (x^{2}y))j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{2}y} \\cdot 2xyi - \\frac{1}{x^{2}y} \\cdot x^{2}j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{x}i - \\frac{1}{y}j \\\\\n- \\nabla f(2, 1) &= -\\frac{2}{2}i - \\frac{1}{1}j \\\\\n&=-i - j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-\\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j$." 13194139535706 false] [17592186124178 1145 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186124179 1100 "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right)$. What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" 13194139535706 false] [17592186124179 1100 "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" 13194139535706 true] [17592186124179 1145 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } g ( x , y ) & = \\langle  \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( \\ln   ( \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}} ) ) , \\, \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( \\ln   ( \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}} ) ) \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} \\cdot  \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2 \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} , \\, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} \\cdot  \\frac{3 x {y}^{2}}{2 \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  \\frac{1}{2 x} , \\, \\frac{3}{2 y} \\rangle \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } g ( \\frac{1}{2} , \\frac{3}{2} ) & = \\langle  \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot  ( \\frac{1}{2} )} , \\frac{3}{2 \\cdot  ( \\frac{3}{2} )} \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  1 , 1 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$\n\nin the direction of\n\n$$\n\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Vert \\nabla g(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{2})\\Vert  &= \\Vert \\langle 1, 1\\rangle \\Vert  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535706 false] [17592186124179 1145 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186124376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362331 13194139535706 true] [17592186124376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362338 13194139535706 true] [17592186124376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362345 13194139535706 true] [17592186124376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362366 13194139535706 true] [17592186124376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362421 13194139535706 true] [17592186124377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362352 13194139535706 true] [17592186124377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362386 13194139535706 true] [17592186124377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362400 13194139535706 true] [17592186124377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362428 13194139535706 true] [17592186124378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362359 13194139535706 true] [17592186124378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362373 13194139535706 true] [17592186124378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362393 13194139535706 true] [17592186124378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362407 13194139535706 true] [17592186124378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362414 13194139535706 true] [17592186124881 1017 #edb/ref 17592186122308 13194139535706 false] [17592186124881 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362380 13194139535706 true] [17592186124881 1100 "In what direction does the function $g(x, y, z) = y^{2} + \\sin (2\\pi x + \\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1, 1, 2)$?" 13194139535706 false] [17592186124881 1100 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,2)?$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186124881 1145 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z),\\,2y,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1,2) & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2),\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi ),\\,2,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩.$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186124881 1145 "The gradient is $$\\nabla g=\\langle2\\pi\\cos(2\\pi x+\\pi z),2y,\\pi\\cos(2\\pi x+\\pi z)\\rangle.$$ At $(1,1,2)$ the cosine is $\\cos4\\pi=1$, so $\\nabla g=\\langle2\\pi,2,\\pi\\rangle$. This nonzero gradient points in the direction of greatest increase." 13194139535706 false] [17592186362331 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362332 13194139535706 true] [17592186362331 1089 "q-130257" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362331 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535706 true] [17592186362331 1100 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j.$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362331 1113 #uuid "4fdbef4a-96b4-5d61-a423-a5d3b28ba07f" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362331 1145 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,1) & =i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇\\,f(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(\\sqrt{3})+2(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362332 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362333 13194139535706 true] [17592186362332 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362334 13194139535706 true] [17592186362332 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362335 13194139535706 true] [17592186362332 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362336 13194139535706 true] [17592186362332 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362337 13194139535706 true] [17592186362332 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535706 true] [17592186362332 1041 "selection" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362332 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362336 13194139535706 true] [17592186362332 1110 #uuid "9c3b339c-645a-5aca-94cb-877c4133c408" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362333 1034 "2\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362333 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535706 true] [17592186362333 1111 #uuid "1e88a357-c5cc-5568-85ae-94bdda9b4ad6" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362334 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+4}{2}" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362334 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535706 true] [17592186362334 1111 #uuid "5cdb3595-7a0a-53db-81b3-618eb321a9fe" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362335 1034 "\\sqrt{3}-1" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362335 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535706 true] [17592186362335 1111 #uuid "4d6c0613-d577-5ca5-be23-3df167fde053" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362336 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362336 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535706 true] [17592186362336 1111 #uuid "8a613c31-f47a-5e9c-9110-8be6de36c91f" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362337 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362337 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535706 true] [17592186362337 1111 #uuid "5e58f2ec-e905-5c15-8ed1-3bdd36b6c30c" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362338 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362339 13194139535706 true] [17592186362338 1089 "q-130260" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362338 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535706 true] [17592186362338 1100 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ at the point $(0,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3\\,i+4\\,j.$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362338 1113 #uuid "b1939235-4888-56a0-832d-3d89089852b9" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362338 1145 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,-1) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,-1) & =∇\\,f(0,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362339 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362340 13194139535706 true] [17592186362339 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362341 13194139535706 true] [17592186362339 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362342 13194139535706 true] [17592186362339 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362343 13194139535706 true] [17592186362339 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362344 13194139535706 true] [17592186362339 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535706 true] [17592186362339 1041 "selection" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362339 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362340 13194139535706 true] [17592186362339 1110 #uuid "2e33e530-7e03-562a-abfe-728c1d513687" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362340 1034 "-\\frac{8}{5}" 13194139535706 true] [17592186362340 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535706 true] [17592186362340 1111 #uuid 